---
# THIS loki (the observability one) has one cross-namespace, in-cluster caller: homelab-mcp, via
# CLAUDE_LOKI_URL — not the plain LOKI_URL homelab-mcp also holds, which points at the different
# loki-homelab service in {{ k8s_namespace }} and is out of scope here. monitor-bridge does not
# call THIS loki (its LOKI_URL / check_loki_ingestion / check_loki_reachable all target that
# other loki-homelab instance) — worth naming explicitly so a future policy written for
# loki-homelab isn't drafted on the assumption that monitor-bridge has no loki caller at all.
#
# grafana, tempo, kube-state-metrics and otel-collector need no per-workload policy — NOT
# because their callers stay inside {{ k8s_observability_namespace }} (loki itself, like
# prometheus and tempo, is also read on host loopback by `otelq`, a workstation host process —
# see claude-otel/CLAUDE.md) but because every non-namespace caller these four have arrives over
# a loopback hostPort, which is admitted by the baseline's node-CIDR `ipBlock` peer
# (`netpol_baseline_obs_node_cidrs`, networkpolicy-observability.yaml.j2), not by anything in
# this file. That ipBlock peer carries no `ports` restriction, so trimming
# `netpol_baseline_obs_node_cidrs` to entries with a same-service-nameable caller would silently
# break otelq's hostPort path on the next flip — grep this comment before touching that list.
#
# INGRESS ONLY, same as the rest of this role: an Egress policy selects pods correctly on this
# cluster and blocks nothing (measured 2026-08-07). Writing one would read like a control in the
# repo and do nothing in the cluster.
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: loki-callers
  namespace: {{ k8s_observability_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: loki
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
{# `| bool`, matching the `when:` in tasks/main.yml — same trap netpol_baseline_obs_enforced
   already guards against for the namespace baseline: a STRING override like
   `-e netpol_baseline_obs_enforced=false` is truthy to bare Jinja and False to Ansible's
   `when:`, so without `| bool` the two sides disagree and this policy would fence loki with no
   probe to catch it. #}
{% if netpol_baseline_obs_enforced | bool %}
    - from:
        # namespaceSelector and podSelector are SIBLING KEYS in ONE list item, so they AND
        # together: homelab-mcp, but only inside {{ k8s_namespace }}. As two separate list items
        # they would OR, admitting the entire {{ k8s_namespace }} namespace (or any pod anywhere
        # labelled homelab-mcp) rather than just this one caller.
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: {{ k8s_namespace }}
          podSelector:
            matchExpressions:
              - key: app
                operator: In
                values: [homelab-mcp]
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 3100
{% else %}
    # OFF. One empty rule admits everything. `ingress: []` would be the exact opposite and deny
    # everything, which is the easiest thing in this file to get backwards.
    - {}
{% endif %}
